 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference time depends on MLP depth and width. For a D6-W60 MLP, inference times range from 15.0 to 25.3 seconds per object on the ShapeNetCoreV1 dataset (Rifle, Chair, Airplane, Sofa, Table) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on MLP size. Configurations like D6-W60 use 60 hidden channels per layer [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the ShapeNetCoreV1 dataset (200 instances each for Rifle, Chair, Airplane, Sofa, Table), the D6-W60 MLP produces meshes with mean face count of 146,827 [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** The method scales with the capacity of the MLP; deeper and wider networks yield higher fidelity but require more computation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Works directly from raw point cloud data without 3D supervision (pre-computed SDFs or occupancy grids) [S6, unofficial]. Produces state-of-the-art surface approximations with higher level-of-details and fidelity compared to previous methods [S6, unofficial]. Implicit regularization favors smooth and natural zero-level set surfaces [S6, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** The zero-level isosurface only approximates the ground-truth mesh; approximation quality depends on MLP capacity [S1, unofficial]. The solution to the Eikonal equation is not unique for point boundary data [S6, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When reconstructing shapes from raw point clouds (with or without normals) and high-fidelity details are required without pre-computed distance fields [S6, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When explicit 3D supervision is available and faster training is preferred (e.g., regression baselines) [S6, unofficial], or when dealing with very thin objects where standard Eikonal regularization may suffer from vanishing gradients (consider Bubble Loss) [S11].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training time depends on the number of epochs, batch size, and MLP depth/width. Inference time for a D6-W60 MLP ranges from 15.0 to 25.3 seconds for mesh extraction (marching cubes) on various ShapeNet objects, with a mean of 20.8 seconds [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is dominated by the MLP parameters and the gradient computation graph during backpropagation. For D6-W60, the mean face count of the extracted mesh is 146,827 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The method produces state-of-the-art surface approximations with higher level-of-detail and fidelity compared to previous methods that require 3D supervision [S6, unofficial]. It suppresses spurious outliers and topological noise with mild Eikonal regularization ($\lambda_{Eik} = 0.02$), accepting a marginal decrease in surface precision (Chamfer Distance/F1) for improved topological consistency [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Works directly from raw point cloud data without pre-computed SDFs or occupancy grids [S6, unofficial]. Implicit regularization favors smooth and plausible zero level sets [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Approximation quality depends on MLP capacity [S1, unofficial]. The solution to the Eikonal equation is not unique for discrete point data [S6, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When training on raw point clouds with or without normals and high-fidelity implicit representations are required without 3D supervision [S6, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time inference is critical and MLP capacity is limited, or when pre-computed SDFs are available and regression is sufficient [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the paper "Implicit Geometric Regularization for Learning Shapes" by Amos Gropp et al. (2020) [S6, unofficial], this method was introduced to compute high-fidelity implicit neural representations directly from raw data. It addresses the limitations of previous methods that required 3D supervision or explicit regularization on the zero level set [S6, unofficial]. The method leverages the theoretical property that optimizing a loss function encouraging the network to vanish on the input point cloud and have a unit norm gradient results in solutions close to a signed distance function with a smooth and plausible zero level set [S6, unofficial]. It has been widely adopted in neural surface rendering and implicit neural representations [S7, S6, unofficial].
+The method was introduced by Amos Gropp et al. in the paper "Implicit Geometric Regularization for Learning Shapes" (ICML 2020) [S6, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of previous implicit neural representations that required 3D supervision (pre-computed SDFs or occupancy grids) [S6, unofficial]. The key insight is that optimizing a loss function encouraging the network to vanish on the input point cloud and have a unit norm gradient possesses an implicit geometric regularization property that favors smooth and natural zero level set surfaces [S6, unofficial]. The method has been used on datasets like ShapeNetCoreV1 (Rifle, Chair, Airplane, Sofa, Table categories) [S1, unofficial] and D-Faust for human scans [S6, unofficial]. It differs from NeRF by using SDF values and Eikonal regularization instead of density and color prediction [S7, unofficial].
 
